[5] Missing-picture synthesis
  The finite data supports a two-switch proof interface:
    switch A: strict Haar mass > 0 gives an open witness interval.
    switch B: strict Haar mass = 0 routes to boundary-owner skeletons.
  AP and GW share the same boundary-owner skeleton, so Haar/Baire alone
  cannot see the H12->D3 transfer.  The transfer must be carried by
  C27/unital labels.
  The Jacobsthal gate then says the only single hidden acceleration site
  compatible with the AP/GW summit is v=12.  Among the 12-branches,
  C27/unital labels split D3 (GW tight boundary) from D9 (open K33 lane).
  Residue impostors such as 12->26 show why covering/divisibility must
  precede raw residue or apex-tournament conclusions.

  Candidate theorem shape:
    after standard reductions, any primitive LRC14 endpoint atom with no
    strict Haar witness has the AP/GW six-pair boundary skeleton; the
    derived gate forces any hidden single acceleration to be 12; C27/unital
    pair completion permits only the D3 tight branch, while D9 and unit
    petals open Haar fronts or feed the K33/state-lift route.

  What remains global:
    prove every bad atom enters this skeleton-gate language, or prove the
    complementary open-front/state-lift discharge for atoms outside it.
